Now I need to understand whether the Angel token is created attacking the right player. In the card code:

```rust
let defender = state.opponent(controller);
if let Some(ref mut combat) = state.combat {
    combat.attackers.insert(token_id, defender);
}
```

The code auto-assigns the Angel token to attack the opponent. But the ruling says:
"You choose which player or planeswalker the Angel token is attacking. It doesn't have to be attacking the same player or planeswalker that Geist of Saint Traft is attacking."

This is a ruling from 2020-08-07, and in a two-player game, there's only one opponent, so auto-selecting the opponent is actually fine. 

In a multiplayer game, the player would need to choose which opponent to attack, but this 2-player implementation sidesteps that. I'm also wondering about planeswalkers—the ruling explicitly mentions you can choose to attack a planeswalker instead of the player, but I need to check if planeswalkers are even implemented in this system, since the opponent function only returns a PlayerId.

For a strictly 2-player game without planeswalker support, auto-targeting the opponent makes sense. But if planeswalkers are supported, the player should get to choose their target. Let me verify what the game actually supports.

